<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leo VI the Wise</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/866">866</a>–912</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Leo the Wise, Leo VI, Léōn ō Sophós · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leo VI the Wise was Byzantine emperor from 886 to 912 and the second ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>, though his parentage is unclear: he was the son either of Michael III or of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>. Reportedly very well read, he completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilika">Basilika</a>, a Greek code in 60 books that translated and arranged the laws of Justinian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, and issued new laws, the Novels, which removed obsolete Roman institutions such as the Senate and the consulate. His reign saw defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> at Boulgarophygon in 896 and the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a>, the last Byzantine outpost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, in 902. His fourth marriage, to Zoe Karbonopsina, caused a scandal; their son became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise.jpg" alt="Leo VI the Wise" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Leo_VI_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The young Constantine VII, son of <b>Leo VI the Wise</b>, became nominal ruler of the Byzantine Empire under a regency council appointed by his dying uncle Alexander and headed by Patriarch Nicholas Mystikos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-6">6 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
